\title{Robots.txt Crawler-Access Restrictions and the Liquidity of Disclosure Windows}
\author{Xueyang Liu\thanks{Universitat Ram\'on Llull. Preliminary draft; please do not circulate.}}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent We study what happens to stock liquidity around mandatory disclosures when a firm restricts automated access to its website through its \texttt{robots.txt} file. Using a staggered difference-in-differences design over 2021--2025, we find that firms that block search and discovery crawlers experience a rise in \emph{abnormal} filing-window Amihud illiquidity---illiquidity in the $[-1,+1]$ trading-day window around 10-K and 10-Q filings, measured relative to a local non-event benchmark. The increase does not reflect lower trading participation or wider posted spreads; instead, filing windows exhibit a stronger return and volume response, and the Amihud increase loads on a thin-day price-impact ``wedge.'' The effect concentrates among younger firms and is accompanied, among firms with pre-existing analyst coverage, by suggestive evidence of greater analyst engagement. We find no evidence that the effect operates through delayed price incorporation, posted-spread widening, EDGAR substitution, or that it is specific to AI/LLM crawlers. The evidence is consistent with a depth / price-impact (Kyle, 1985) interpretation rather than a single identified channel.
\end{abstract}

