%==============================================================================
\section{Institutional Background: Robots.txt and Machine-Mediated Discovery}\label{sec:bg}
%==============================================================================

\subsection{The Robots Exclusion Protocol}\label{sec:bg_rfc}
The Robots Exclusion Protocol, standardized as RFC~9309 \citep{RFC9309}, lets a website operator publish a \texttt{robots.txt} file specifying which automated clients (``crawlers,'' identified by a \emph{User-agent} token) may fetch which URL paths. A rule group beginning \texttt{User-agent: *} applies to all crawlers; a group naming a specific crawler applies only to it; and a \texttt{Disallow:} directive removes the listed path (\texttt{Disallow: /} removes the entire site). Compliance is voluntary but near-universal among the major search and discovery operators, so a \texttt{robots.txt} restriction is a meaningful constraint on how a firm's public web content---including the investor-relations pages that host and link disclosure materials---can be crawled, indexed, and surfaced by machine intermediaries.

We track a fixed population of \textbf{31 search and discovery crawlers}, listed in Table~\ref{tab:crawlers}. These are the user agents whose stated purpose is to crawl the open web for search, indexing, or answer/retrieval surfaces. A firm is coded as restricting a crawler in a given month if its \texttt{robots.txt} disallows that crawler at the site root or on a sub-path, either through a rule group naming the crawler or through an applicable site-wide wildcard group (RFC~9309 longest-match, allow-on-tie). We treat all 31 crawlers symmetrically here; whether the effect differs across crawler types is examined in Section~\ref{sec:mech_notai}. For the research design, what matters is the restriction of automated, search-capable access.

\input{tab_crawlers}

\subsection{Why Do Firms Restrict Crawlers?}\label{sec:bg_why}
Whether crawler restrictions can be treated as plausibly exogenous to a firm's liquidity depends on \emph{why} firms adopt them. Three descriptive facts, shown in Figures~\ref{fig:adoption}--\ref{fig:pathanatomy}, indicate that the typical restriction reflects content-agnostic site administration rather than a deliberate attempt to suppress investor information.

First, adoption is \emph{blanket and gradual} rather than event-driven. Figure~\ref{fig:adoption} plots cumulative adoption among 14{,}615 trackable firms: the median first-restriction month is February~2021, and the ``blocks any crawler'' curve sits far above the ``specifically names a crawler'' curve throughout, so most adoption is site-wide rather than crawler-targeted.

Second, restrictions are overwhelmingly implemented as site-wide wildcards. Figure~\ref{fig:wildcard} shows that of the 9{,}528 firms that restrict at least one crawler, \textbf{82.9\%} (7{,}902 firms) do so through a \texttt{User-agent: *} wildcard that applies to every crawler at once; explicit per-crawler naming is a small and uneven minority.

Third, the path-specific restrictions that do exist land on \emph{site hygiene}, not investor content. Following the website path-category dictionaries of \citet{BoullandLee2023}, Figure~\ref{fig:pathanatomy} classifies the path-level restrictions of 2{,}171 treated firms: \textbf{2{,}396} target hygiene/operations paths (CMS admin, query-string parameters, file extensions, media assets, carts, careers), versus only \textbf{96} on investor-relevant content (investor relations, news/press, ESG, legal). The treatment therefore reflects broad machine-access restriction, not targeted removal of investor-relations content.

Together these patterns motivate the identifying assumption: conditional on firm and time fixed effects, the timing of a crawler restriction is plausibly unrelated to contemporaneous shocks to the firm's disclosure-window liquidity. The residual threat is \emph{selection}---firms that restrict may differ from those that do not---which we address through the staggered design, pre-trend tests, and the robustness checks of Section~\ref{sec:results}.

