%==============================================================================
\section{Data and Sample}\label{sec:data}
%==============================================================================

\subsection{Sample and Sources}\label{sec:data_sample}
The unit of observation is a firm filing event---a 10-K or 10-Q filed with the SEC over 2021--2025. We obtain filing dates from EDGAR, daily returns and dollar volume from CRSP, accounting fundamentals from Compustat, and analyst coverage from I/B/E/S. \texttt{robots.txt} histories are reconstructed monthly for each firm's web host. The estimation sample comprises \textbf{27{,}327} filing events from \textbf{1{,}908} firms---\textbf{722} crawler-restricting (treated) firms and \textbf{1{,}186} never-restricting (control) firms with a valid filing-window observation.

\subsection{Independent Variable: Crawler-Access Restriction}\label{sec:data_iv}
Let $\mathit{Block}_i$ equal one if firm $i$ ever restricts any of the 31 crawlers of Table~\ref{tab:crawlers} during the sample, and let $m^{\ast}_i$ denote firm $i$'s first-restriction month. The staggered treatment indicator for filing event $f$ of firm $i$ is
\begin{equation}
\bigl(\mathit{Block}\times\mathit{Post}\bigr)_{i,f}
= \mathbf{1}\!\left[\,\mathit{Block}_i=1 \;\text{and}\; m(f) > m^{\ast}_i\,\right],
\end{equation}
where $m(f)$ is the filing's calendar month. We exclude the firm's filing in its first-restriction month itself (to avoid contaminating the onset), and we restrict the treated group to firms with a clean pre-restriction history (first restriction on or before 2025-06, the sample end). Control firms (``never-restricting'') never restrict any of the 31 crawlers.

\subsection{Dependent Variable: Abnormal Filing-Window Illiquidity}\label{sec:data_dv}
Our liquidity measure is the daily Amihud \citeyearpar{Amihud2002} illiquidity ratio. For firm $i$ on trading day $d$,
\begin{equation}
\mathit{AIM}_{i,d} \;=\; \frac{\lvert r_{i,d}\rvert}{\mathit{DolVol}_{i,d}}\times 10^{6},
\end{equation}
where $r_{i,d}$ is the daily return and $\mathit{DolVol}_{i,d}$ is daily dollar volume (price $\times$ share volume); days with non-positive dollar volume are set to missing. Higher $\mathit{AIM}$ means a given dollar of trading moves the price more, i.e.\ lower liquidity.

We measure liquidity in the disclosure window \emph{relative to a local non-event benchmark}, following the abnormal-announcement-window methodology of \citet{Beaver1968}, \citet{LandsmanMaydew2002}, and \citet{LandsmanMaydewThornock2012}. With day $0$ defined as the first trading day on or after the filing date, the event window is $[-1,+1]$ trading days---the announcement-window convention of \citet{LandsmanMaydewThornock2012}---and the benchmark is the local non-event window $[-60,-10]$ trading days. Our primary outcome is the within-event abnormal log Amihud,
\begin{equation}
\mathit{AbnAIM}_{i,f}
= \ln\!\Bigl(1+\overline{\mathit{AIM}}^{\,[-1,+1]}_{i,f}\Bigr)
- \ln\!\Bigl(1+\overline{\mathit{AIM}}^{\,[-60,-10]}_{i,f}\Bigr),
\end{equation}
with a level variant $\mathit{AbnAIM}^{\mathrm{lev}}_{i,f}=\overline{\mathit{AIM}}^{\,[-1,+1]}_{i,f}-\overline{\mathit{AIM}}^{\,[-60,-10]}_{i,f}$. Differencing against the firm's own local window removes the firm's contemporaneous liquidity level, so the outcome isolates the \emph{abnormal} disclosure-window component. The $[-1,+1]$ event window follows \citet{LandsmanMaydewThornock2012}; the specific 50-day benchmark $[-60,-10]$ is chosen to sit close to the event yet avoid overlap with the disclosure window (and with the adjacent-quarter filing), and is not attributed to a particular study. We require at least 2 valid days in the event window and 30 in the benchmark, and winsorize the outcome at the 1st/99th percentiles within the estimation sample.

\subsection{Controls}\label{sec:data_ctrl}
Firm-level controls are log total assets, leverage, return on assets, market-to-book, asset tangibility, and analyst coverage ($\ln(1+\text{number of estimates})$), each winsorized at 1/99 and mean-centered within the estimation sample.

\subsection{Descriptive Statistics}\label{sec:data_desc}
Table~\ref{tab:summ} reports means and standard deviations by treatment group. Treated and control firms are broadly comparable; treated firms are slightly smaller (log assets 7.07 vs.\ 7.32) and carry marginally more analyst coverage. Both groups have a slightly negative average abnormal log Amihud, reflecting a generally improving liquidity trend over the sample; the treatment effect is identified from the treated-post differential, not the unconditional mean.

\input{tab_summary}

%==============================================================================
\section{Empirical Strategy}\label{sec:strategy}
%==============================================================================
Our baseline is a staggered two-way fixed-effects (TWFE) difference-in-differences specification:
\begin{equation}
\mathit{AbnAIM}_{i,f}
= \beta\,\bigl(\mathit{Block}\times\mathit{Post}\bigr)_{i,f}
+ \gamma'\mathbf{X}_{i,f}
+ \alpha_i + \delta_{m(f)} + \varepsilon_{i,f},
\label{eq:twfe}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_i$ are firm fixed effects, $\delta_{m(f)}$ are filing-month fixed effects, $\mathbf{X}$ collects the controls, and standard errors are clustered by firm. Because treatment onset is staggered, the TWFE estimator can be contaminated by heterogeneous-timing weights \citep{GoodmanBacon2021,deChaisemartinDHaultfoeuille2020}. We therefore report alongside it the imputation estimator of \citet{BorusyakJaravelSpiess2024} (``BJS''), which estimates fixed effects on untreated observations only and is robust to treatment-effect heterogeneity; the BJS specification also delivers a 12-period pre-trend test, which we report for every outcome. The de~Chaisemartin--D'Haultf\oe uille \citeyearpar{deChaisemartinDHaultfoeuille2024} estimator is used as a further robustness check. Because the outcome is already an abnormal (within-event difference) measure, it is pre-trend-clean even where the underlying levels are not (Section~\ref{sec:results}).

%==============================================================================
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
%==============================================================================
Table~\ref{tab:main} reports the main effect. Restricting search and discovery crawlers raises abnormal filing-window illiquidity. In logs, the TWFE estimate is $+0.0109$ ($p=0.033$) without controls and $+0.0111$ ($p=0.029$) with controls; the BJS estimate is $+0.0134$ ($p=0.010$) with a clean pre-trend ($p=0.48$). In levels, the effect is $+0.033$ to $+0.035$ (TWFE, $p<0.05$) and $+0.039$ under BJS ($p=0.007$, pre-trend $p=0.38$). The two estimators agree closely, and the BJS pre-trends do not reject parallel trends, supporting a causal reading of the abnormal-window effect.

We emphasize that this is an \emph{abnormal}, relative effect. As we show in the identification appendix, the filing-window illiquidity \emph{level} does not rise; rather, the firm's local benchmark Amihud declines after restriction while the disclosure window does not improve commensurately, so disclosure windows become relatively more liquidity-constrained than the local benchmark. We therefore describe the effect throughout as an increase in abnormal, benchmark-relative illiquidity, not as filing windows becoming illiquid in absolute terms.

\input{tab_main}

%==============================================================================
\section{Why Does Abnormal Filing-Window Illiquidity Increase?}\label{sec:mech}
%==============================================================================
This section asks what the deterioration is and what it is not. We do not claim to identify a single mediating channel; rather, we document a market signature, locate where the effect is strongest, and rule out the leading alternatives.

\subsection{Market Signature: A Stronger Information Response, Not Less Trading}\label{sec:mech_sig}
Table~\ref{tab:decomp} decomposes the abnormal Amihud into its moving parts. Restriction is followed by higher abnormal return variance (AVAR $+0.121$, $p=0.015$) and higher absolute abnormal returns ($+0.054$, $p=0.025$), and by higher---not lower---dollar volume ($+0.061$, $p=0.020$), so the deterioration is not a collapse in trading participation. In the tradition of \citet{Beaver1968}, \citet{KimVerrecchia1994}, and \citet{LandsmanMaydewThornock2012}, rising return variance and volume around a disclosure indicate a stronger, more heterogeneously-interpreted information response.

The key cell is the ratio-of-means Amihud, which captures a \emph{uniform} proportional shift in price impact per dollar: it is economically near zero and statistically insignificant ($-0.003$, $p=0.90$), providing no support for a uniform price-impact shift. The abnormal Amihud increase is instead carried by the dispersion ``wedge'' between the mean of daily ratios and the ratio of means ($+0.038$, $p=0.048$)---price discovery becomes \emph{lumpier}, with sharper price impact on thin-volume days. We treat the wedge as a corroborating but fragile cell (it only just clears the 5\% threshold), and anchor the signature on AVAR and the ratio-of-means null; we report dollar volume as decomposition accounting (it is the Amihud denominator and works against, not toward, the illiquidity rise).

\input{tab_decomp}

\subsection{Where the Effect Is Strongest: Younger Firms}\label{sec:mech_locus}
Table~\ref{tab:locus} interacts the treatment with predetermined firm characteristics. The effect concentrates among \emph{younger} firms: the interaction of treatment with log firm age is negative and significant (log outcome $-0.019$, $p=0.031$; level $-0.056$, $p=0.025$) and survives joint controls for analyst coverage, EDGAR attention, and size, as well as separate horse races against institutional ownership, retail float, and filing-package size. We read age as a condition---an immature information environment---rather than as a separate causal channel: it is a cross-sectional moderator estimated by TWFE, with no parallel-trends test on the interaction, and the young-treated cell is thin.

\input{tab_locus}

\subsection{A Human-Intermediary Response Among Covered Firms}\label{sec:mech_analyst}
Among firms with pre-existing analyst coverage we find suggestive---not conclusive---evidence of broader analyst engagement, and no evidence of analyst delay. The number of distinct analysts updating around filings rises ($+0.090$, $p=0.015$), with a pre-trend that only just clears our bar ($p=0.10$); the related analyst update-days measure is positive but its pre-trend falls just short ($p=0.099$), so we do not count it as a clean channel. Response speed, if anything, increases rather than slows ($-1.45$ days, $p=0.021$). This pattern is the opposite sign to \citet{ChangLjungqvistTseng2023}, who find that \emph{raising} the cost of accessing mandatory disclosures leads analysts to retreat; here, among already-covered firms, the more information-intensive filing window appears to draw somewhat \emph{more} human-intermediary engagement. Because this is estimated only on the analyst-covered subsample---which does not coincide with the young-firm locus---it cannot account for the headline effect, and we present it as a complementary observation, not a validated channel.

\subsection{What the Effect Is \emph{Not}}\label{sec:mech_notai}
Table~\ref{tab:excl} collects the excluded channels.

\emph{Not delayed price incorporation.} A post-filing drift test over $[+2,+10]$ shows no delayed incorporation: the delayed-response share is a precise null (BJS $-0.002$, $p=0.73$, pre-trend $p=0.51$), and signed drift is null. We claim only this specific exclusion---the test bounds the $[+2,+10]$ drift, not slowing within $[-1,+1]$ or beyond $+10$.

\emph{Not posted-spread widening or lower participation.} Posted quoted spreads and two low-frequency spread proxies \citep{CorwinSchultz2012,AbdiRanaldo2017} are null, and trading activity rises rather than falls (Section~\ref{sec:mech_sig}). The illiquidity is Amihud / price-impact, not a posted-spread channel.

\emph{Not disclosure substitution.} Abnormal EDGAR access to the treated firms' filings, measured in the same abnormal form, is null across periodic, 10-K, and 8-K access (BJS $p>0.45$). Investors do not compensate by reading the filings directly; there is no substitution channel.

\emph{Not AI/LLM-specific.} This is the only place we separate AI from traditional crawlers. The AI-search crawlers in our universe---OAI-SearchBot (OpenAI), Claude-SearchBot (Anthropic), PerplexityBot (Perplexity), and DuckAssistBot (DuckDuckGo), with an expanded set adding YouBot, Andibot, and Amzn-SearchBot---form a small subset of the 31. Restricting attention to firms that name these AI crawlers yields underpowered, insignificant estimates (e.g.\ AI-strict level $-0.06$, $p=0.30$), while a non-AI placebo arm of low-reliance traditional crawlers is itself significant ($+0.049$, $p=0.05$). Because most firms restrict every crawler at once through a wildcard (Figure~\ref{fig:wildcard}), the data cannot attribute the effect to AI crawlers specifically. We therefore describe the treatment as a restriction on \emph{machine-mediated discovery in general}, following restrictions on search-capable crawler access---not as an AI-driven effect.

\emph{Not template churn or IR-page targeting.} The effect survives controls for the type of path restricted; firms whose restriction is a query-string/CMS-template pattern show, if anything, a weaker effect, and the result is broad across path types rather than concentrated in investor-content paths (consistent with Figure~\ref{fig:pathanatomy}).

\input{tab_excl}

\subsection{Interpretation}\label{sec:mech_interp}
Assembling the pieces, the deterioration is best read as a depth / price-impact phenomenon in the spirit of \citet{Kyle1985}: a stronger, lumpier information response with sharper price impact on thin-volume days, concentrated where information environments are immature, with every adjacent alternative (delayed diffusion, analyst delay, spreads, volume, substitution, AI-specificity) excluded. Because no positive first-stage measure of discoverability validates (see Appendix), we state this as \emph{consistent with} a price-impact interpretation, not as a single identified mediator.

