\appendix
\section{Robustness and Identification}\label{app:rob}
%==============================================================================
\subsection{The headline is a benchmark-relative, pre-trend-clean effect}\label{app:eventspec}
Because the outcome is a within-event difference between the filing window and a local benchmark, it is worth asking which leg moves. Table~\ref{tab:eventspec} regresses the two levels separately on the treatment. The filing-window absolute level does \emph{not} rise---it is slightly negative and fails its own parallel-trends test ($p\approx0.004$)---while the local benchmark level declines with a clean pre-trend. The positive headline is therefore the benchmark level falling faster than the filing window: disclosure windows do not share in the firm's broadly improving local liquidity. Only the abnormal difference (the headline) is pre-trend-clean (log $p=0.48$, level $p=0.38$), which is why we build inference on it and describe the effect throughout as benchmark-relative rather than as an absolute rise in filing-window illiquidity.

\input{tab_eventspec}

\subsection{The effect is not an earnings-announcement artifact}\label{app:earnings}
Because periodic filings often accompany earnings announcements, a natural concern is that the filing-window effect is really an earnings-announcement effect. Table~\ref{tab:earnings} addresses this directly. The effect is not concentrated in filings that coincide with the earnings announcement (Panel~A): the concurrent cell, despite being the best-powered, is the weakest. When we drop all earnings-concurrent filings and re-estimate the headline on the non-concurrent subsample (Panel~B), the effect survives---TWFE $+0.0102$ ($p=0.029$), BJS $+0.0072$ ($p=0.043$) with a clean pre-trend---and adding an earnings-window indicator to the matched-sample headline does not absorb it (Panel~C). The effect is individually significant among the more frequent quarterly (10-Q) disclosures and not among annual (10-K) filings, but the two are not statistically distinguishable, so we read this as a frequency pattern, not a form-specific mechanism.

\input{tab_earnings}

\subsection{Heterogeneity-robust estimation}\label{app:did}
Our primary defense against the staggered-timing biases of two-way fixed effects \citep{GoodmanBacon2021,deChaisemartinDHaultfoeuille2020} is the \citet{BorusyakJaravelSpiess2024} imputation estimator, reported alongside TWFE in every results table; its estimates track TWFE closely and its pre-trend tests do not reject parallel trends for the headline or the market-signature components. For the few-treated-cluster crawler-type arms of Section~\ref{sec:mech_notai}, we confirm inference with a wild-cluster bootstrap (Webb weights), under which the significant non-AI placebo arm survives. The \citet{deChaisemartinDHaultfoeuille2024} estimator is reported as a further check. \textit{[dCDH point estimates to be inserted from the rerun.]}

\subsection{The Common-Crawl discoverability first stage does not validate}\label{app:cc}
We attempted a direct first-stage test of whether restriction reduces a firm's machine-crawlable footprint, using monthly Common Crawl URL coverage. It does not validate, for two independent reasons. The static post-restriction coefficient has the \emph{wrong sign} (coverage rises: $+0.41$, $p=0.002$ in the cleanest stratum), and the event study shows a significant \emph{negative} pre-trend ($-0.25$, $p=0.008$), rejecting parallel trends. The diagnosis is a construct mismatch: Common Crawl's own crawler (CCBot) is not among the 31 treated crawlers, and only 184 of 7{,}258 firms (2.5\%) ever restrict CCBot, so restricting the treated crawlers does not mechanically reduce Common Crawl coverage. We report this for transparency and do not rely on it; the paper makes no claim to a validated positive first stage.

